---

<CaseSection id="context" standfirst="Monk AI Group runs AI audits for clients; every audit asset was written by hand from the same transcripts.">
  <p>
    An audit at Monk AI Group runs department by department: discovery calls, then findings, solution ideas, ROI, a
    report, a deck, a scope of work and demos. Every one of those assets was written by hand, mostly by the delivery
    lead and me, from the same raw transcripts. In the audits themselves I asked the quantitative questions on calls,
    came up with solutions, built the demos and the deck, and presented the validation.
  </p>
  <dl class="users">
    <div><dt>Delivery lead</dt><dd>Reviews drafts and signs off each ROI figure.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Sign-off reviewer</dt><dd>Approves what goes to the client.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Me</dt><dd>Admin, architect and demo owner.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Development team</dt><dd>Reads the spec packs for each chosen solution.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="problem" standfirst="Numbers lived in prose across many documents and drifted apart.">
  <p>
    When a new call or a correction arrived, someone had to remember every place the old information lived, and nobody
    could do that reliably. In one real estate audit, the same impact figure appeared as three different ranges in
    three documents: one of the estimates behind it was corrected, and the change did not reach them all.
  </p>
  <Stats
    items={[
      { value: "3 ranges", label: "for one impact figure, across three documents of the same audit", source: "OP:10; DS:63" },
      { value: "By hand", label: "every asset, from prep documents to the scope of work, written from the same transcripts", source: "OP:9" },
    ]}
  />
  <p>Real audits showed other ways facts went wrong on the way from a call to a document:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Transcripts misspell the names of the client's systems.</li>
    <li>The interviewer's words get attributed to the client.</li>
    <li>Ideas the consultant floated come back as if the client had asked for them.</li>
    <li>Numbers change during the call itself.</li>
    <li>The scope of work and its appendix number the same modules differently.</li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="research"
  standfirst="A pipeline I had already built and used, then a close study of two complete real audits."
>
  <ul>
    <li>
      In November 2025 I built the first automated version: an n8n pipeline with an intake agent before the call and
      four analysis agents after it, with an optional consolidation step. It was first used on a yacht management
      client's audit.
    </li>
    <li>
      For the redesign I went through two complete real audits: their contracts, both final presentations, five
      discovery transcripts, the questionnaire answers and the demo code.
    </li>
    <li>
      On 29 July 2026 the first build plan went through an internal review, and its findings became version 1.1, the
      plan the redesign follows.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Diagram
    n={1}
    diagram={firstVersion}
    caption="The first automated version, November 2025. One transcript in, four analysis files out, with one human checkpoint."
  />
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="decisions"
  standfirst="The system holds the facts, every fact keeps its source, a separate checker verifies it, and people keep every judgement."
>
  <Decision title="The system holds the facts">
    <p>
      Documents stop being the place where numbers live. They become views of one audit graph, each stamped with the
      data it came from, so a change lands once and flows everywhere.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Every quote and number keeps its source">
    <p>
      Each fact points to the speaker turn, the exact words and the timestamp it came from. A corrected transcript
      re-links the anchors, and any that cannot be re-linked become a task for a person.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="An independent checker">
    <p>
      Every agent that writes facts has a separate checker that defaults to refusal: a quote must be verbatim in the
      source, and a number must have been said by the client, not suggested by the interviewer.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Updates without anyone orchestrating them">
    <p>
      When new material arrives, newer figures supersede older ones, overwritten ideas are revised or retired, and only
      the documents that depend on the change are redone. A passing remark never overwrites a number the client
      confirmed: that becomes a conflict for a person to decide.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="People keep the judgement">
    <p>
      Queues can earn autonomy by measured performance, but ROI figures, rankings, report sections, the deck, the scope
      of work and anything sent to a client are never approved automatically. The system may propose retiring a
      solution; it never does it.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Plain language everywhere">
    <p>
      Every name a person sees has to pass a zero-training test, agents included: Source Cleaner and Fact Checker, not
      mythology. Tasks read as sentences, such as "a new figure contradicts a client-confirmed number: pick which one
      stands".
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="A client portal, voice agents and automated client capture" notBuilt>
    <p>
      Excluded by decision. The system automates the audit as it already runs, with files uploaded by hand first, and
      leaves anything client-facing to people.
    </p>
  </Decision>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="solution" standfirst="An audit graph with an update engine at its heart, agents that draft and check, and human gates.">
  <Diagram
    n={2}
    diagram={redesign}
    caption="The redesign, July 2026. Facts are checked on the way in; only what depends on a change is redone; people approve at the gates."
  />
  <h3>Architecture notes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A Next.js app on Cloudflare Workers, with the database on D1, source files on R2 and jobs on Queues.</li>
    <li>
      Agents call the Anthropic API with structured outputs, and the model is chosen by stakes: a fast model for
      classification, the default for most steps, the most capable for fact checking and drafting reports.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every step is cached on a fingerprint of its inputs and prompt version, so unchanged inputs return identical
      output and a reviewer sees only the one number that changed.
    </li>
    <li>Every agent call is logged with its model, prompt version, tokens, cost and time taken.</li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="outcome" standfirst="Planned and checked against two real audits in July 2026, started, then cancelled.">
  <p>
    The plan was complete at the end of July 2026 and was checked against the material of two real audits. The first
    build sessions ran from 30 July to 1 August 2026: deployment, sign-in, file parsing, and loading both audits'
    material. Then the project was cancelled.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="learned" standfirst="Fix where the number lives, not the documents.">
  <p>
    Fix where the number lives, not the documents. Once the facts sit in one place, a correction lands once instead
    of being chased through every file.
  </p>
  <p>
    Automate the updating and keep the judgement with people. Whatever writes a draft should not also check it.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="role" standfirst="I built the first version, then designed the redesign as its architect and owner.">
  <p>
    I built the first automated version in November 2025. For the redesign I was the architect and owner: the build
    plan names me as its admin, architect and demo owner. The two requirements at its centre were mine: everything stays
    current when new material arrives, and every name a person sees is in plain language.
  </p>
</CaseSection>
